{"name":"Wooded Acre","hex":"#b59b7e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wooded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered with trees.","Aged in wooden casks."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of wood"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wooded"},{"term":"acre","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An English unit of land area (symbol: a. or ac.) originally denoting a day's ploughing for a yoke of oxen, now standardized as 4,840 square yards or 4,046.86 square metres.\n\n An area of 10,240 square yards or 4 quarters.","An area of 10,240 square yards or 4 quarters."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acre"}],"entries":[{"title":"Woodland","blocks":[{"text":"A woodland is, in the broad sense, land covered with woody plants, or in a narrow sense, synonymous with wood, a low-density forest forming open habitats with plenty of sunlight and limited shade. Some savannas may also be woodlands, such as savanna woodland, where trees and shrubs form a light canopy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Woodland%2C_Bogside_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2215308.jpg/330px-Woodland%2C_Bogside_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2215308.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodland"},{"title":"Acre","blocks":[{"text":"The acre is a unit of land area used in the British imperial and the United States customary systems. It is traditionally defined as the area of one chain by one furlong, which is exactly equal to 10 square chains, 1⁄640 of a square mile, 4,840 square yards, or 43,560 square feet, and approximately 4,047 m2, or about 40% of a hectare. The acre is sometimes abbreviated ac, but is usually spelled out as the word \"acre\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Acre_superimposed_over_football_fields.svg/330px-Acre_superimposed_over_football_fields.svg.png?utm